            "56": "1) Baroque (This period, spanning roughly from 1600 to 1750, is characterized by ornate and complex musical forms, prominent use of basso continuo, and the works of Bach, Handel, and Vivaldi exemplify this style.)",
            "57": "4) Huckleberry Finn\nThe protagonist and narrator of *Adventures of Huckleberry Finn* is Huckleberry Finn. The story is told entirely from his perspective, and he is the central character navigating the moral and social issues of the novel.",
            "58": "2) Michelangelo, David",
            "59": "2) Atlas (Atlas was the Titan condemned by Zeus to hold up the heavens as punishment for siding with Cronus in a rebellion against the Olympian gods.)",
            "60": "4) Carmen\n\nThe 'Habanera' (\"L'amour est un oiseau rebelle\") is one of the most famous arias in the opera *Carmen* by Georges Bizet.",
            "61": "4) Prime number\nA prime number is a natural number greater than 1 that has no positive divisors other than 1 and itself. Composite numbers have factors other than 1 and themselves, rational numbers can be expressed as a fraction, and irrational numbers cannot be expressed as a fraction.",
            "62": "4) Central Processing Unit\n\nThe CPU stands for Central Processing Unit. It is the primary component of a computer that carries out instructions and performs calculations.",
            "63": "4) 12\nThe square root of a number is a value that, when multiplied by itself, equals the original number. 12 multiplied by 12 equals 144.",
            "64": "2) 1010\nThe binary number 1010 is equivalent to the decimal number 10 because:\n\n1010₂ = (1 * 2³) + (0 * 2²) + (1 * 2¹) + (0 * 2⁰) = 8 + 0 + 2 + 0 = 10.",
            "65": "2) 180 degrees\n\nThe sum of the interior angles of any triangle is always 180 degrees. This is a fundamental theorem in Euclidean geometry.",
            "66": "1) Python (Python was created by Guido van Rossum and released in 1991.)",
            "67": "4) 120\n5! = 5 * 4 * 3 * 2 * 1 = 120",
            "68": "2) Hypertext Transfer Protocol\n\nThe Hypertext Transfer Protocol is the foundation of data communication on the World Wide Web. It defines how messages are formatted and transmitted between web browsers and web servers.",
            "69": "4) Octagon\nAn octagon is a polygon with eight sides. The other options have fewer or more than eight sides.",
            "70": "4) Pythagoras",
            "71": "2) Temporary short-term memory\n\nRAM (Random Access Memory) provides a fast, temporary storage space for data that the computer is actively using. It allows the CPU to quickly access information needed for running programs and performing tasks, unlike permanent storage like a hard drive or SSD, which stores data long-term.",
            "72": "4) 24\nThe least common multiple (LCM) of 4 and 6 is the smallest positive integer divisible by both. We can find the LCM by listing multiples of each number until we find the smallest common multiple. Multiples of 4 are: 4, 8, 12, 16, 20, 24, 28... Multiples of 6 are: 6, 12, 18, 24, 30... The smallest common multiple is 24.",
            "73": "2) Alan Turing\nAlan Turing is widely recognized as the father of modern computer science and artificial intelligence due to his conceptualization of the Turing machine, his work on the theoretical limits of computation (the Turing test), and his foundational contributions to AI research.",
            "74": "4) Pi (π)\nPi (π) represents the ratio of a circle's circumference to its diameter. It is a fundamental constant in geometry and mathematics, approximately equal to 3.14159.",
            "75": "3) Queue\nA queue operates on a First-In, First-Out (FIFO) basis, meaning the first element added to the queue is the first one to be removed. This is in contrast to a stack, which follows a Last-In, First-Out (LIFO) principle. Trees and graphs are not based on FIFO principles, and neither are stacks.",
            "76": "1) 0\nA horizontal line has a constant y-value and no change in x-value. This represents a slope of zero.",
            "77": "2) Android Inc.\n\nAndroid Inc. developed the Android operating system before Google's acquisition in 2005. They initially created the system as a mobile operating system for smartphones and tablets.",
            "78": "4) 5/36\nThere are 36 possible outcomes when rolling two standard 6-sided dice. We need to find the number of outcomes that result in a sum of 7. These outcomes are: (1,6), (2,5), (3,4), (4,3), (5,2), and (6,1). There are 6 such outcomes. Therefore, the probability of rolling a sum of 7 is 6/36, which simplifies to 5/36.",
            "79": "3) Secure",
            "80": "4) False\nThe result of a boolean AND operation is only True if both operands are True. If either operand is False, the result is False.",
            "81": "4) 11\nIn a standard soccer match, each team fields eleven players, including the goalkeeper.",
            "82": "2) Parasite \n\nThe 92nd Academy Awards (2020) recognized \"Parasite\" as the Best Picture winner, marking the first time a non-English language film achieved this honor.",
            "83": "1) Knight(The Knight is the only piece in chess that can move in an 'L' shape and leap over other pieces.)",
            "84": "3) Brazil\nThe Summer Olympic Games were held in Rio de Janeiro, Brazil in 2016.",
            "85": "2) Vincent van Gogh \n\nVincent van Gogh painted 'The Starry Night' while residing at the Saint-Paul-de-Mausole asylum in Saint-Rémy-de-Provence. He was a patient there and created the iconic painting during his time there.",
            "86": "1) Love\nThe term \"love\" in tennis represents a score of zero points within a game. It is a traditional term derived from the French word \"amour,\" meaning \"love.\"",
            "87": "3) The Beatles\nThe Beatles released 'Abbey Road' in 1969, a landmark album known for its iconic cover and complex musical arrangements.",
            "88": "4) 26.2 miles\nThe standard distance of a full marathon is 26.2 miles. This distance was chosen in 1921 by Fred Lebow, founder of the New York City Marathon, to make the race more challenging and memorable.",
            "89": "3) SpongeBob SquarePants\nSpongeBob SquarePants is the animated character who lives in a pineapple under the sea, as established by the show's premise.",
            "90": "3) 3 points\nA successful field goal made from beyond the three-point arc in basketball is worth three points.",
            "91": "3) Batman\nBatman is famously known as the \"Dark Knight\" of Gotham City, a title earned through his detective skills, martial arts prowess, and brooding persona.",
            "92": "2) Emmy awards the excellence in US television broadcasting.\n\nThe Emmy Awards are specifically designed to recognize outstanding achievements in television production and performance. The Grammy Awards recognize musical achievements, the Oscars recognize film achievements, and the Tony Awards recognize theatrical achievements.",
            "93": "2) Croatia",
