            "17": "2) Adriatic Sea\nThe Adriatic Sea is specifically located between the Italian Peninsula and the Balkan Peninsula, separating them and acting as a natural barrier.",
            "18": "3) Treaty of Versailles\nThe Treaty of Versailles was the treaty that officially ended World War I in 1919. It imposed harsh terms on Germany, including territorial losses, disarmament, and heavy reparations, contributing to the instability that ultimately led to World War II.",
            "19": "1) Ho Chi Minh City\nThe city was formerly known as Saigon during the period of French colonial rule and before the establishment of the current Vietnamese state. Ho Chi Minh City is the current and official name.",
            "20": "2) Vatican City\nVatican City is the smallest independent country in the world by both land area (approximately 0.44 square kilometers) and population (less than 800 residents). Monaco is larger, San Marino is larger, and Liechtenstein is larger than Vatican City.",
            "21": "3) Venus\nVenus has the highest surface temperature in our solar system due to its extremely dense atmosphere, which creates a runaway greenhouse effect trapping heat.",
            "22": "2) Carbon",
            "23": "2) Brain (The brain, despite its relatively small size, is the human organ that consumes the largest percentage of the body's total energy budget at rest due to its constant need for electrical activity and maintaining homeostasis.)",
            "24": "4) 300,000 km/s\nThe speed of light in a vacuum is approximately 299,792,458 meters per second. 300,000 km/s is the closest rounded value provided in the multiple-choice options.",
            "25": "1) Nitrogen (Nitrogen makes up approximately 78% of Earth's atmosphere by volume. This is due to its abundance in the Earth's crust and its role as a primary component of the air we breathe. Carbon dioxide, argon, and oxygen make up significantly smaller percentages of the atmosphere.)",
            "26": "2) Igneous rocks are formed from the cooling and solidification of magma or lava.",
            "27": "2) Au Gold is represented by the chemical symbol Au, which comes from the Latin word \"aurum\" meaning gold.",
            "28": "4) Chloroplast\nThe chloroplast is the organelle within plant cells that contains chlorophyll and is responsible for carrying out photosynthesis, the process of converting light energy into chemical energy in the form of sugars.",
            "29": "1) Diamond(Diamond is the hardest naturally occurring substance on Earth.)",
            "30": "1) 206\nThe standard adult human body contains 206 bones. This number varies slightly between individuals due to minor variations in bone fusion and the presence of sesamoid bones (small bones embedded in tendons).",
            "31": "2) Albert Einstein\nAlbert Einstein developed the theory of general relativity, which describes gravity as a curvature of spacetime caused by mass and energy.",
            "32": "1) 7.0\nPure distilled water is neutral at 25°C. Neutral water has a pH of 7.0, which is defined as the pH where the concentration of hydrogen ions (H+) and hydroxide ions (OH-) are equal. This is the standard reference point for measuring acidity or alkalinity.",
            "33": "4) Gravity\nGravity is the force of attraction between any two objects with mass. The Sun's immense mass exerts a gravitational force on the planets, keeping them in their orbital paths. The other forces – the strong, weak, and electromagnetic forces – operate at the subatomic level and are not responsible for large-scale phenomena like planetary orbits.",
            "34": "4) Omnivores\nThese animals obtain nutrients from a varied diet consisting of both plant and animal sources. Herbivores eat only plants, carnivores eat only meat, and detritivores consume dead organic matter.",
            "35": "3) Electron\nThe electron is the subatomic particle that carries a negative electric charge. Neutrons and protons are electrically neutral, while positrons are the antiparticle of the electron and have a positive charge.",
            "36": "3) O negative\nO negative blood has antigens that are present on all red blood cells, but lacks A, B, and Rh antigens. This makes it compatible with all other blood types, hence its designation as the universal red blood cell donor.",
            "37": "3) Ohm",
            "38": "3) Crocodilians\nCrocodilians (reptiles) share a more recent common ancestor with birds than the other options. Birds evolved from a group of theropod dinosaurs, and crocodilians represent a lineage closely related to these dinosaurs, making them the closest surviving relatives.",
            "39": "1) Thermosphere\nThe thermosphere is the layer of the atmosphere that lies directly above the troposphere. It is characterized by increasing temperatures with altitude and is where the International Space Station orbits.",
            "40": "3) Mercury (Mercury has the shortest day due to its rapid rotation.)",
            "41": "1) Leo Tolstoy (Leo Tolstoy was the author of 'War and Peace', a monumental work exploring Russian society during the Napoleonic Wars.)",
            "42": "2) Michelangelo\nMichelangelo painted the ceiling of the Sistine Chapel, renowned for its frescoes depicting scenes from the Book of Genesis.",
            "43": "3) Denmark\nHamlet is the Prince of Denmark, a key element of the play's setting and plot. The entire conflict revolves around the political machinations within the Danish court.",
            "44": "3) Claude Monet",
            "45": "3) George Orwell\nThe novel '1984' was written by George Orwell, who was a British novelist, essayist, journalist, and critic. It is a seminal work of dystopian literature, exploring themes of totalitarianism, surveillance, and psychological manipulation.",
            "46": "2) Moby-Dick\nThe novel *Moby-Dick* by Herman Melville famously begins with the line \"Call me Ishmael,\" establishing the narrator and the story's trajectory.",
            "47": "4) Salvador Dalí\nSalvador Dalí is widely recognized as the artist who created \"The Persistence of Memory\" with its iconic melting clocks, a cornerstone of surrealist art.",
            "48": "4) The monster created from corpses",
            "49": "3) Antonio Vivaldi\nAntonio Vivaldi composed 'The Four Seasons', a set of four violin concertos depicting different seasons of the year. It is one of his most famous and influential works.",
            "50": "3) The Odyssey\nThe Odyssey is the epic poem by Homer that recounts Odysseus' arduous ten-year journey back to Ithaca after the Trojan War. The Iliad focuses on the events of the Trojan War itself, while The Theogony describes the origins of the Greek gods, and The Aeneid tells the story of Aeneas, a Roman hero.",
            "51": "1) Robert Frost (Robert Frost wrote 'The Road Not Taken'. This poem is arguably his most famous work, exploring themes of choice and individuality. Langston Hughes was a prominent figure in the Harlem Renaissance, Walt Whitman is known for his expansive free verse, and Emily Dickinson is celebrated for her concise and unconventional poetry.)",
            "52": "2) Piano (A standard modern piano has 88 keys, which are used to produce a wide range of musical notes and harmonies.)",
            "53": "2) The Lord of the Rings",
            "54": "4) Pointillism\nPointillism is defined by the meticulous application of tiny dots of color that, when viewed from a distance, blend optically to create an image.",
            "55": "1) Charlotte Brontë\nThe novel *Jane Eyre* was written by Charlotte Brontë under the pseudonym Currer Bell to avoid societal expectations regarding female authorship at the time.",
            "56": "1) Baroque",
            "57": "4) Huckleberry Finn \n\nThe protagonist and narrator of *Adventures of Huckleberry Finn* is Huckleberry Finn. The story is told entirely from his perspective, and he is the central character navigating the moral and social issues of the novel.",
            "58": "2) Michelangelo, David",
            "59": "2) Atlas (The Titan Atlas was condemned by Zeus to hold up the heavens as punishment for his role in the Titanomachy, the war between the Titans and the Olympian gods.)",
            "60": "4) Carmen",
            "61": "4) Prime number\nA prime number is defined as a natural number greater than 1 that has no positive divisors other than 1 and itself. Composite numbers have factors other than 1 and themselves, rational numbers can be expressed as a fraction, and irrational numbers cannot be expressed as a fraction.",
            "62": "4) Central Processing Unit",
            "63": "4) 12\nThe square root of a number is a value that, when multiplied by itself, equals the original number. 12 multiplied by 12 equals 144.",
            "64": "4) 1011\nThe binary number 1011 is equivalent to the decimal number 10. In binary, each digit represents a power of 2, starting from the rightmost digit as 2^0. So, 1011 = (1 * 2^3) + (0 * 2^2) + (1 * 2^1) + (1 * 2^0) = 8 + 0 + 2 + 1 = 11. However, the question asks for the binary number equivalent to 10. Let's convert 10 to binary:\n10 / 2 = 5 remainder 0\n5 / 2 = 2 remainder 1\n2 / 2 = 1 remainder 0\n1 / 2 = 0 remainder 1\nReading the remainders from bottom to top, we get 1010.\nLet's re-examine the options:\n1) 1001 = (1 * 2^3) + (0 * 2^2) + (0 * 2^1) + (1 * 2^0) = 8 + 0 + 0 + 1 = 9\n2) 1010 = (1 * 2^3) + (0 * 2^2) + (1 * 2^1) + (0 * 2^0) = 8 + 0 + 2 + 0 = 10\n3) 1100 = (1 * 2^3) + (1 * 2^2) + (0 * 2^1) + (0 * 2^0) = 8 + 4 + 0 + 0 = 12\n4) 1011 = (1 * 2^3) + (0 * 2^2) + (1 * 2^1) + (1 * 2^0) = 8 + 0 + 2 + 1 = 11\nThe correct answer is 1010.\nI apologize for the previous incorrect response. Let's re-evaluate the options.\n1) 1001 = 8 + 1 = 9\n2) 1010 = 8 + 2 = 10\n3) 1100 = 8 + 4 = 12\n4) 1011 = 8 + 2 + 1 = 11\n\nThe correct answer is 2) 1010.\nI made an error in my initial conversion. Let's verify:\n10 in binary is 1010.\n1011 is 8 + 2 + 1 = 11.\nTherefore, the correct answer is 2) 1010.\nI apologize for the repeated errors. Let's be precise.\n\nThe correct answer is 2) 1010.\n10 in decimal is 1010 in binary.\n1010 = (1 * 2^3) + (0 * 2^2) + (1 * 2^1) + (0 * 2^0) = 8 + 0 + 2 + 0 = 10.\nThe other options are incorrect.",
            "65": "2) 180 degrees\nThe sum of the interior angles of any triangle is always 180 degrees. This is a fundamental theorem in Euclidean geometry.",
            "66": "1) Python (Guido van Rossum created Python in 1991 as a successor to the ABC language, and it was first released to the public in 1991.)",
            "67": "4) 120\nFactorial (n!) is the product of all positive integers less than or equal to n. Therefore, 5! = 5 * 4 * 3 * 2 * 1 = 120.",
            "68": "2) Hypertext Transfer Protocol\n\nThe Hypertext Transfer Protocol is the foundation of data communication on the World Wide Web. It defines how messages are formatted and transmitted between web browsers and web servers. The other options are incorrect and do not accurately represent the function of HTTP.",
            "69": "4) Octagon\nAn octagon is a polygon with eight sides. A heptagon has seven sides, a hexagon has six sides, and a nonagon has nine sides.",
            "70": "4) Pythagoras",
            "71": "2) Temporary short-term memory\n\nRAM (Random Access Memory) provides a fast, temporary storage space for data that the computer is actively using. It allows the CPU to quickly access information needed for running programs and performing tasks, unlike permanent storage like a hard drive or SSD, which stores data long-term.",
            "72": "4) 24\nThe least common multiple (LCM) of 4 and 6 is the smallest positive integer divisible by both. The prime factorization of 4 is 2^2 and the prime factorization of 6 is 2 * 3. The LCM is 2^2 * 3 = 4 * 3 = 12. However, 12 is not in the options. Let's re-examine the options. 1) 18 is divisible by 6 but not 4. 2) 16 is divisible by 4 but not 6. 3) 12 is divisible by both 4 and 6. 4) 24 is divisible by both 4 and 6. Therefore, 24 is the correct answer.",
            "73": "2) Alan Turing\nAlan Turing is widely recognized as the father of modern computer science and artificial intelligence due to his pivotal work on the theoretical foundations of computation (the Turing machine), his conceptualization of the Turing test for machine intelligence, and his contributions to early AI research.",
            "74": "4) Pi (π)\nThe mathematical constant representing the ratio of a circle's circumference to its diameter is Pi (π). Pi (π) is defined as the ratio of a circle's circumference to its diameter, approximately equal to 3.14159. Tau (τ), the golden ratio (phi), and Euler's number (e) are all different mathematical constants with distinct definitions.",
            "75": "3) Queue\nA queue operates on a First-In, First-Out (FIFO) basis, meaning the first element added to the queue is the first one to be removed. This is in contrast to a stack, which follows a Last-In, First-Out (LIFO) principle. Trees and graphs are not based on FIFO operations, and neither are stacks.",
            "76": "1) 0\nA horizontal line has a constant y-value and no change in x-value. In a Cartesian coordinate plane, the slope is defined as the change in y divided by the change in x (rise over run). Since there is no change in x (run), the slope is 0.",
            "77": "2) Android Inc.\n\nAndroid Inc. developed the Android operating system before Google's acquisition in 2005. Google recognized the potential of the platform and subsequently took full control, rebranding and expanding its development efforts. The other options were not involved in the creation of Android.",
            "78": "4) 5/36\nThere are 36 possible outcomes when rolling two standard 6-sided dice. We need to find the number of outcomes that result in a sum of 7. These outcomes are: (1,6), (2,5), (3,4), (4,3), (5,2), and (6,1). There are 6 such outcomes. Therefore, the probability of rolling a sum of 7 is 6/36, which simplifies to 5/36.",
            "79": "3) Secure\nHTTPS stands for Hypertext Transfer Protocol Secure. The \"S\" indicates that the connection is secured using encryption, protecting data transmitted between a web browser and a web server.",
            "80": "4) False\nThe result of a boolean AND operation is only True if both operands are True. If either operand is False, the result is False. Therefore, True AND False evaluates to False.",
            "81": "4) 11\nEleven players are on the field for one team during a standard soccer match. This includes one goalkeeper and ten outfield players.",
            "82": "2) Parasite \n\nThe 92nd Academy Awards (2020) recognized \"Parasite\" as the Best Picture winner, marking a historic moment as the first non-English language film to achieve this honor.",
            "83": "1) Knight(The Knight is the only piece in chess that can move in an 'L' shape and leap over other pieces. Its movement is unique among all the other pieces.)",
            "84": "3) Brazil\nThe 2016 Summer Olympic Games were held in Rio de Janeiro, Brazil.",
            "85": "2) Vincent van Gogh\nVincent van Gogh painted 'The Starry Night' while residing at the Saint-Paul-de-Mausole asylum in Saint-Rémy-de-Provence.",
            "86": "1) Love\nThe term \"Love\" in tennis represents a score of zero points within a game. It originates from the French word \"l'oeuf\" (egg), which was used to represent nothing.",
            "87": "3) The Beatles (Abbey Road)\nThe Beatles released 'Abbey Road' in 1969, making it their critically acclaimed album.",
            "88": "4) 26.2 miles\nThe standard distance of a full marathon is 26.2 miles. This distance was established in 1908 for the first Olympic marathon and has remained the standard ever since.",
            "89": "3) SpongeBob SquarePants\nSpongeBob SquarePants is the animated character who resides in a pineapple under the sea, as established by the Nickelodeon television series of the same name.",
            "90": "3) 3 points\nA successful field goal made from beyond the three-point arc in basketball is worth three points.",
            "91": "3) Batman, the Dark Knight of Gotham City",
            "92": "2) Emmy awards are annually given to honor excellence in US television broadcasting.\n\nThe Emmy Awards are specifically designed to recognize outstanding achievements in television and radio programming. The Grammy Awards recognize musical achievements, the Oscar Awards recognize film achievements, and the Tony Awards recognize theatrical achievements.",
